    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 301805\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1105 AM PDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Precipitation chances and cooler temperatures return today \n  and continue through the weekend.\n\n* Cool and dry conditions for early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Thursday. \n\nMoisture from former TS Vance will continue to stream north \novernight, with dewpoints currently in the 50s over the lower \nColorado River Valley and generally mid-40s to around 50 south of I-\n15. This plume will push further inland through the day along with \nincreasing shower and thunderstorm chances. Light radar returns have \nbeen observed overnight across southern Nevada, likely associated \nwith a weak vort max riding up along the moisture boundary. A slight \nchance of light showers and an isolated thunderstorm remains before \nsunrise across southern Nevada, eastern San Bernardino, and western \nMohave counties; however, recent HRRR runs have backed off on this \npotential.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances will ramp up this afternoon, \nespecially across Mohave County where the greatest instability \nresides. Convection here is expected to be more widespread, and with \nPWATs around 1.00\" to 1.25\", localized flash flooding will be \npossible. Storms may also develop further west into Clark and \nLincoln counties, though coverage will be more scattered with \nlocalized downpours. Areas further west should remain dry through \nthe day.\n\nConditions dry out Friday night with only isolated showers possible \nSaturday. As the primary trough off the California coast pushes \ninland late Saturday night into Sunday, more widespread \nprecipitation will overspread the region. Forecast impacts and rain \ntotals remain somewhat uncertain given sensitivity to the system's \nexact track: a more progressive, aggressive wave spreads \nprecipitation across the entire forecast area, whereas a slower \ntrack shunts moisture east and keeps activity largely confined to \nArizona. Current guidance suggests the lower valleys could see \nbetween 0.25\" and 0.50\", with Mohave County potentially picking up \naround an inch. With limited cold air aloft and the core of the \ntrough staying over northern Nevada, snow levels will remain high \n(generally above 9,0009,500 feet). The highest peaks of the Spring \nMountains could pick up several inches of snow, but recreational \nareas like Lee Canyon should see little to no accumulation.\n\nAs the trough exits early next week, a tightening surface gradient \nalong its western flank will drive breezy to gusty winds Monday, \nparticularly across the southern Great Basin. Most gusts will \nsubside Monday evening, though terrain-channeled winds through the \nColorado River Valley could linger into Tuesday. Cooler air will \nsettle in behind the system despite shortwave ridging building \noverhead, keeping highs roughly 5 degrees below seasonal normals \nthrough Tuesday under dry conditions.\n\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nsouth winds build into the late morning with a pause in rain \nchances. Breezier winds arrive in the mid-afternoon from the south- \nsouthwest with shower activity increasing across the valley. \nCHances for storms will be possible in the lines of showers, and \ncould result in gustier winds and cloud bases as low as 6000 ft AGL \nalong with chances for lightning. Storm chances decrease into the \nevening and winds ease into the night while turning westerly, but \nchances for showers linger through the night and much of Saturday \nmorning. Showers exit late that morning with winds turning easterly. \nBreezy southeast winds arrive into Saturday afternoon. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Showers continue to move \nacross the Las Vegas Valley and the Colorado River Valley, with some \nquickly-passing, non-impactful rain chances for the rest of the TAF \nsites. Moderate to gusty south to west winds arrive this afternoon. \nAs winds increase,  storms and better chances for showers arrive for \nall but KDAG and KBIH. Storm chances ease in the late afternoon and \ninto the evening, but shower chances look to linger into the night. \nWInds reduce into the evening and overnight, becoming light across \nmuch of the region.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
